          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Peter Higgs</strong><br>\nPeter Higgs was born in Newcastle upon Tyne, England, on May 29, 1929. His early interest in physics was inspired by the discoveries of the early 20th century, like quantum mechanics and Einstein's theory of relativity. Higgs excelled in mathematics and physics from a young age, which led him to study at King's College, London. He later pursued a PhD, which set the stage for his groundbreaking work in particle physics.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Higgs Mechanism</strong><br>\nIn 1964, Peter Higgs proposed the existence of a particle that would eventually bear his name: the Higgs boson. This particle was part of his theory, now known as the Higgs mechanism. It explains how particles acquire mass in the Standard Model of particle physics. The Higgs mechanism has been integral to understanding the fundamental forces that shape our universe.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Collaboration and Publication</strong><br>\nAlthough Higgs conducted much of his research independently, he was part of a vibrant scientific community. His findings were published around the same time as other physicists who were working on similar concepts. This period was marked by intense research activity and collaboration, which helped refine the theories describing particle interactions.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
